<edge source="us_iran_gulf_escalation::kinetic_engagement" target="global_bond_repricing::long_term_yield_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in direct kinetic exchanges raises geopolitical risk and drives a near-term flight-to-safety into long-dated sovereign bonds, pushing their yields down as prices rise.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_gulf_escalation</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_repricing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_iran_gulf_escalation::kinetic_engagement" target="global_oil_reordering::oil_export_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Direct strikes on tankers, terminals and related marine infrastructure destroy export capacity and force port/terminal closures and cargo diversions, immediately reducing volumetric exports.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_gulf_escalation</data>
  <data key="d40">global_oil_reordering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_iran_gulf_escalation::kinetic_engagement" target="global_oil_reordering::geopolitical_conflict_in" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased armed exchanges escalate threat perceptions among regional and external powers, triggering diplomatic confrontations, military posturing and allied responses that raise overall geopolitical conflict intensity.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_gulf_escalation</data>
  <data key="d40">global_oil_reordering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_iran_gulf_escalation::kinetic_engagement" target="russia_ukraine_war::coordination_info_sharin" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained increases in US/Iran kinetic exchanges prompt Washington and NATO to reassign ISR platforms, intel-liaison officers and analytic capacity to the Gulf, directly reducing the tempo and volume of institutionalized intelligence exchanges and joint planning support available to Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_gulf_escalation</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_iran_gulf_escalation::kinetic_engagement" target="russia_ukraine_war::military_procurement_rat" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in kinetic activity in the Gulf creates urgent demand for Western munitions, air-defence and naval stores that leads governments and producers to reallocate existing stocks and near-term production runs toward Middle East partners, delaying acquisition deliveries and new contracts destined for Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_gulf_escalation</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_iran_gulf_escalation::kinetic_engagement" target="us_china_strategic_rival::geo_tech_tension" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained increases in kinetic engagements in the Gulf prompt immediate US-led policy responses (expanded sanctions, tightened export controls and stricter vetting of dual‑use suppliers) that are explicitly applied to cross-border technology flows and third‑party vendors, producing a measurable rise in techno‑nationalist frictions with China.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_gulf_escalation</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
</edge>
