<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geo_tech_tension" target="global_bond_repricing::cross_border_flows" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating trade controls, export restrictions and sanctions elevate compliance and political risk, prompting portfolio managers and allocators to withdraw capital from affected jurisdictions' fixed-income markets and reallocate into safer or friendlier markets, reducing net cross-border bond flows.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_repricing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geo_tech_tension" target="global_bond_repricing::long_term_yield_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Acute US–China tech friction drives a flight-to-quality into developed-market sovereigns (notably US Treasuries), boosting demand for long-duration safe assets and putting downward pressure on long-term yields.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_repricing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geo_tech_tension" target="global_oil_reordering::legal_sanctions_regime_s" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising US–China tech tensions prompt rapid adoption and tightening of export controls and targeted sanction statutes to block cross‑border transfers of AI hardware, software and services, expanding the scope and enforceability of legal sanctions frameworks.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">global_oil_reordering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geo_tech_tension" target="global_oil_reordering::oil_export_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalation in US–China strategic rivalry leads state buyers and national oil companies to reallocate purchasing away from suppliers aligned with the other side (via informal boycotts, preferred‑supplier contracts or credit restrictions), reducing export volumes from those targeted producers and rerouting flows to politically allied exporters.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">global_oil_reordering</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geo_tech_tension" target="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_financial" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Rises in US–China tech friction prompt U.S. and allied policymakers to expand export controls and secondary financial sanctions targeting intermediaries and firms that enable Sino-Russian technology transfers, raising aggregate sanctions pressure related to the war.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geo_tech_tension" target="us_iran_gulf_escalation::kinetic_engagement" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising US–China techno-nationalist friction reduces crisis-management and accelerates state and proxy arms transfers (including ISR, strike drones and missiles) to Gulf actors, enabling and incentivizing higher-frequency and higher-intensity maritime strikes and exchanges.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_gulf_escalation</data>
</edge>
