<edge source="global_oil_reordering::political_authority_stre" target="global_bond_repricing::policy_rate_expectations" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A measurable increase in political authority in major energy producers lowers the perceived probability of disruptive policy or supply shocks (and associated upside inflation risk), leading markets to scale back expectations of imminent central‑bank tightening.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_oil_reordering</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_repricing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_oil_reordering::political_authority_stre" target="russia_ukraine_war::coordination_info_sharin" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Stronger centralized political control in major oil producers enables unilateral sale and diplomatic choices (bilateral deals, non-cooperation on enforcement) that reduce the willingness of those states to participate in allied intelligence-sharing and joint enforcement efforts.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_oil_reordering</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
